INT. PUBLIC POOL - SWIM MEET - EVENING

Chlorine haze. Echoing whistles. Lane ropes in red and white. Parents crowd the bleachers with foam fingers.

COACH TREVOR PIKE, 40s, in a wind jacket and a stopwatch lanyard, kneels at the edge of lane four, speaking low. At his feet, DANI KOWALCZYK, 17, in a cap and goggles, rocks on her heels.

PIKE
She'll be on your left. Don't watch her.

DANI
I won't.

PIKE
You will.

DANI
I will.

He grins and squeezes her shoulder.

Across the deck, in lane five, ANNELIESE VEGA, 17, shakes out her arms. Her coach says something. She answers without looking. Her eyes are on Dani.

Dani looks back. Neither blinks.

PIKE
(quietly)
She beat your time last week.

DANI
I know.

PIKE
She's going to start fast.

DANI
So will I.

The starter walks to the end of the deck. A hush spreads over the bleachers.

STARTER (O.S.)
Swimmers, take your marks.

Dani steps onto the block. Anneliese steps onto hers. They bend. A gun cracks.

Both dive. The water closes over them like glass.

Pike jogs along the side, timer in fist. Dani and Anneliese surface together and churn, white water, arms rising in perfect rhythm.

At the halfway turn, they flip within a breath of each other. A girl in the stands screams.

A TEENAGE TIMEKEEPER, 15, in a borrowed volunteer shirt, appears beside Pike with a clipboard, wide-eyed.

TIMEKEEPER
Coach, which one is yours?

PIKE
The one on the left.

TIMEKEEPER
They're both on the left.

PIKE
Then the one who's winning.

She grins. The swimmers surge toward the wall. Anneliese reaches first by half a hand. Dani slaps the tile a hair behind.

The scoreboard blinks. Both girls hang from the lane rope, gasping, staring at the numbers. Anneliese raises one fist. Dani turns her face to the water, and the goggles hide her eyes.
